From 75ccb1dcad5b79fb4b5f6719a44fdeae623c8bd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bench <bench@local>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 03:17:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 42/43] perf(field): lower-latency ADX Montgomery mul and
 split-reduction squaring

MUL (ADX): CIOS where every product row and every reduction step ends with
both carry chains flushed into its (provably non-overflowing) top limb and the
next one restarts them with a zeroing xor. The steps then depend on each other
only through the limb registers, not through a single flag chain that runs
through all limbs of every round, so the serial path per round is
t[i] -> k_i -> k_i * p[0..1] -> t[i+1]. k_i is computed with imulq (only the
low half is needed; 3-cycle latency instead of 4 and one uop fewer than
mulxq). Fewer instructions overall (no separate flag bookkeeping).

SQR (ADX): same cross-product/doubling/square phases, then the reduction
works on the low half of the square only: U = (T_lo + K*p) / R via four
reduction steps, result = U + T_hi. The reduction does not wait for the
high half of the product and the high limbs no longer sit on the
reduction's carry chains.

Output is bit-identical: the reduction digits k_i are the unique digits of
K < R with T + K*p = 0 mod R, so (T + K*p) / R does not depend on the
schedule. Checked against the portable montgomery_mul on 40M random and
edge-case inputs in [0, 2p) for BN254 Fr and Fq.

Microbench (this VM, 3.9 GHz, cycles per op):
  mul: throughput 47 -> 45, latency 63 -> 54
  sqr: throughput 48 -> 42, latency 72 -> 60
